{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.BallotNumbers","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.BallotNumbers","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Nat.Choose.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Nat.Choose.Vandermonde","lean:module:Mathlib.Combinatorics.Enumerative.Catalan","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.NatAntidiagonal","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Positivity"],"declarations":19,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ballot_sq_sum_catalan","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ballot_sq_sum_catalan","kind":"theorem","line":426,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L426-L442","doc":"**G5, general `n`: `Σ_{j ≤ ⌊n/2⌋} m_j(n)² = catalan n`.**\n\nThe two-row Specht dimensions squared sum to the Catalan number, for every `n`,\nagainst Mathlib's own `catalan`. Equivalently — though the proof never uses the\nequivalence — 321-avoiding permutations of `n` are counted by `C_n`.\n\nCombined with the Wedderburn diagonalisation of the Markov-trace Gram form and\nthe Wenzl vanishing `y_λ = 0 ↔ ℓ(λ) > 2` in convention D, this gives\n`corank(n) = n! − catalan n` for all `n`, superseding the four measured values.\nThe corank statement itself remains conditional on the Wenzl weight\nidentification, which is carried as a Lean class and is therefore invisible to\n`#print axioms` (bean `qou-fpjy`); *this* theorem is unconditional. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ballot_sq_sum_fold","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ballot_sq_sum_fold","kind":"theorem","line":372,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L372-L424","doc":"**The fold: the full-range sum is exactly twice the half-range sum.**\n\nSplit `range (n+2)` at `⌊n/2⌋ + 1`, reindex the tail to start at `0`, reverse\nit with `Finset.sum_range_reflect`, and apply `ballot_reflect` term by term: the\ntail becomes `Σ_{j < n+1−⌊n/2⌋} m_j(n)²`, a *head* sum again.\n\nThe parity split is where the two cases genuinely differ, and it is not an\nartefact of the index choice:\n\n* `n = 2m`: the tail has length `m + 1 = ⌊n/2⌋ + 1`, matching the head exactly.\n* `n = 2m+1`: the tail has length `m + 2`, one term too long. The excess term is\n  `m_{m+1}(2m+1)²`, and `ballot_boundary_zero` says it vanishes.\n\nSo both cases give tail = head, hence full = 2·head. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ballot_reflect","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ballot_reflect","kind":"theorem","line":333,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L333-L370","doc":"**The reflection: `m_{n+1−k}(n) = −m_k(n)` for `k ≤ n+1`.**\n\nBinomial symmetry, read on the difference: `C(n,n+1−k) − C(n,n−k)` is\n`C(n,k−1) − C(n,k)`. The two boundary cases are the ones the uniform argument\nmisses — `k = 0` (where `m_0 = 1` is pinned and `m_{n+1} = −C(n,n) = −1` is\ncomputed) and `k = n+1` (the mirror of the same pair) — so both are discharged\nseparately.\n\nThis is what halves the full-range sum. It also explains the shape of\n`ballot_boundary_zero`: an odd `n` puts a fixed point at `k = (n+1)/2`, where\nthe reflection forces `m_k = −m_k`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ballot_sq_sum_full","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ballot_sq_sum_full","kind":"theorem","line":290,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L290-L331","doc":"**The full-range square-sum: `Σ_{k=0}^{n+1} m_k(n)² = 2·catalan n`.**\n\nThis is the step that makes G5 a closed-form evaluation rather than an\ninduction. Over the *full* range the summand expands as\n`C(n,k+1)² − 2·C(n,k+1)·C(n,k) + C(n,k)²`, and Vandermonde evaluates all three\npieces: `C(2n,n) − 1`, `C(2n,n+1)`, and `C(2n,n)`. The stray `−1` is cancelled\nexactly by the `k = 0` term `m_0(n)² = 1`, which is peeled off separately\nbecause `m_0` is pinned rather than computed. What remains is\n`2·(C(2n,n) − C(2n,n+1))`, and `catalan_eq_choose_sub` identifies that as\n`2·catalan n`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.catalan_eq_choose_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.catalan_eq_choose_sub","kind":"theorem","line":259,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L259-L288","doc":"**`catalan n = C(2n,n) − C(2n,n+1)`**, over `ℤ`.\n\nWritten with `C(2n, n+1)` rather than the more familiar `C(2n, n−1)`. The two\nare equal by binomial symmetry, but `n+1` involves no truncated `ℕ`\nsubtraction, so this form is correct at `n = 0` as well (`1 − 0 = 1 = catalan 0`)\nand needs no side condition. The `n−1` spelling would give `C(0,0) − C(0,0) = 0`\nthere — the same off-by-one that `ballot` itself is defined to avoid.\n\nProof: `(n+1)·catalan n = C(2n,n)` is Mathlib's\n`succ_mul_catalan_eq_centralBinom`, and `(n+1)·C(2n,n+1) = n·C(2n,n)` is\n`Nat.choose_succ_right_eq` at `k = n`. Subtract and cancel `n+1 ≠ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.choose_sq_sum_shift","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.choose_sq_sum_shift","kind":"theorem","line":244,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L244-L257","doc":"**The shifted square-sum**, `Σ_{j ≤ n} C(n,j+1)² + 1 = C(2n,n)`.\n\nBoth sides are the same range-`(n+2)` sum read two ways: peeling the *last*\nterm (`C(n,n+1)² = 0`) reduces it to `choose_sq_sum`, and peeling the *first*\n(`C(n,0)² = 1`) reduces it to the shifted sum. Stated in `ℕ` with the `+ 1` on\nthe left so that no truncated subtraction appears. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.choose_mul_succ_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.choose_mul_succ_sum","kind":"theorem","line":224,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L224-L242","doc":"**Vandermonde, off-diagonal range form.** `Σ_{j ≤ n} C(n,j+1)·C(n,j) = C(2n,n+1)`.\n\nSame transport as `choose_sq_sum`, with one extra step: the `k = 0` term of the\nantidiagonal sum is `C(n,0)·C(n,n+1) = 0`, and peeling it is what shifts the\nindex so that `C(n, n+1−k)` becomes `C(n, j)` against `C(n, j+1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.choose_sq_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.choose_sq_sum","kind":"theorem","line":209,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L209-L222","doc":"**Vandermonde, range form.** `Σ_{k ≤ n} C(n,k)² = C(2n,n)`.\n\n`Nat.add_choose_eq` gives this over `Finset.antidiagonal`; the work is\ntransporting it to `Finset.range` and applying `Nat.choose_symm` to turn\n`C(n, n−k)` into `C(n,k)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ballot_sq_sum_catalan_small","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ballot_sq_sum_catalan_small","kind":"theorem","line":135,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L135-L156","doc":"**G5 at the levels the folio has measured.** `Σ_j m_j(n)² = catalan n` for\n`n = 3, 4, 5`, against Mathlib's own `catalan` rather than a table in this repo.\n\nThese three are now **corollaries** of `ballot_sq_sum_catalan` below, which\nproves the identity for general `n`. They are kept, and kept proved by\n`norm_num` on the literal values rather than by specialising the general\ntheorem, precisely so that they remain an independent check on it: a sign or\nindex error in the general proof would have to reproduce these three numbers by\ncoincidence to survive.\n\nHistorical note, since the pricing was wrong twice. `qou-u2jg` called this \"pure\ncombinatorics, no q\", as though easy; `qou-0cyu` corrected that to \"by RSK, and\nMathlib has neither RSK nor pattern avoidance — LARGE\", offering a direct\ninduction on `ballot_succ_succ` as route (b). Route (b) does not close either\n(the ballot recursion is linear, the Catalan recursion is a convolution). What\ncloses it is reflection plus Vandermonde, with no induction and no tableau. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ballot_values_five","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ballot_values_five","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L131-L133","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ballot_values_four","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ballot_values_four","kind":"theorem","line":127,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L127-L129","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ballot_values_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ballot_values_three","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L121-L125","doc":"**The row sums that the folio actually uses**, checked against the values\n`qou-8iru` measured: the two-row Specht dimensions at `n = 3, 4, 5` are\n`(1,2)`, `(1,3,2)` and `(1,4,5)`, whose squares sum to `5`, `14` and `42`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ballot_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ballot_one","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L116-L119","doc":"`m_1(n) = n − 1` for `n ≥ 1`: the shape `(n−1, 1)` has `n − 1` standard\nfillings. Stated on `ℤ` so the `n = 0` case needs no side condition. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ballot_zero_eq_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ballot_zero_eq_one","kind":"theorem","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L113-L114","doc":"The first column is all ones: `m_0(n) = 1`, the single tableau of shape `(n)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ballot_boundary_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ballot_boundary_zero","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L90-L111","doc":"**The boundary vanishing: `m_{j+1}(2j+1) = 0`.**\n\n`C(2j+1, j+1) = C(2j+1, j)` by binomial symmetry, so the ballot number just past\nthe diagonal is zero. Small, and it is precisely what makes G2's induction close\nin the even case.\n\n**Why it is the crux.** Running `[2]ⁿ⁺¹ = [2]·Σ_j m_j(n)[n−2j+1]` through the\nthree-term recursion and reindexing produces a sum over `j = 0 … ⌊n/2⌋ + 1`,\nwhile the target for `n+1` runs to `⌊(n+1)/2⌋`. Those agree when `n` is odd. When\n`n` is even, say `n = 2m`, the derived sum has one term too many — at\n`j = m + 1`, with coefficient `m_{m+1}(2m+1)`. This lemma says that coefficient\nis `0`, so the extra term disappears and the two sums coincide.\n\nSo the parity split in G2 is not an artefact of a clumsy index choice; it is\nreal, and this is the fact that resolves it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ballot_succ_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ballot_succ_succ","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L73-L88","doc":"**The ballot recursion — the engine.** `m_{j+1}(n+1) = m_{j+1}(n) + m_j(n)`.\n\nTwo cases, each Pascal. The `j = 0` case is the one that would break under a\nnaive uniform definition: `m_1(n+1) = n` while `m_1(n) + m_0(n) = (C(n,1) − 1) + 1`,\nand those agree only because `m_0` was pinned to `1` rather than computed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ballot_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ballot_succ","kind":"theorem","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L70-L71","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ballot_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ballot_zero","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L68-L68","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.ballot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ballot","kind":"def","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/BallotNumbers.lean#L59-L66","doc":"**Ballot number** `m_j(n) = C(n,j) − C(n,j−1)`, the number of standard Young\ntableaux of shape `(n − j, j)`.\n\nThe `j = 0` case is given directly rather than by the difference: `C(n, 0−1)`\nwould be `C(n, 0)` under `ℕ` subtraction, making `m_0(n) = 0` instead of `1`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}